<h2 id="driving-and-delivery">Driving and delivery around the Mile High City</h2>
<p>If you're comparing Denver side hustles in 2026, app work is still the fastest way to earn, but the sprawl punishes random driving. Pick your zones and your hours.</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="rideshare">1. Rideshare driving</h3>
    <p>Denver International Airport sits far out on the plains, so airport trips are long and usually well paid. Combine early-morning DIA runs with post-game pickups around Ball Arena and Coors Field, Friday and Saturday nights in LoDo, RiNo and LoHi, and the slow crawl out of Red Rocks after a show. Our <a href="/lyft-uber/">Lyft and Uber guide</a> covers sign-up and vehicle requirements.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Typically $18–$30/hr before gas and wear</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Eligible car, insurance</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Early birds and night owls</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="delivery">2. Food and grocery delivery</h3>
    <p>DoorDash, Uber Eats, Instacart and Grubhub stay busy across Capitol Hill, Cherry Creek and the Highlands, where dense apartments mean short trips. Snowy evenings are the best-paying nights of the year because nobody wants to drive. Downtown, an e-bike beats a car on both parking and gas.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> Typically $16–$25/hr before gas</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Car, bike or e-bike</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Flexible evenings</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>
<p>Driving costs add up quickly at altitude and on I-25 traffic. Plug in your real miles and gas price before you commit:</p>
