<h2 id="colorado-taxes">Colorado taxes and Denver rules for side income</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> Colorado uses a flat rate, 4.4% for 2025 returns. Under the state's revenue rules the rate can drop in years with large surpluses, so confirm the current figure with the Colorado Department of Revenue.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit, on top of federal income tax. Our <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> estimates your set-aside.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax licenses:</strong> Denver is a home-rule city that collects its own sales tax, so if you sell taxable goods you typically need both a Colorado sales tax license and a Denver one.</li>
  <li><strong>Short-term rentals:</strong> Denver requires a short-term rental license, allows STRs only in your primary residence, and expects lodger's tax to be collected. Platforms won't list unlicensed Denver properties.</li>
  <li><strong>Occupational privilege tax:</strong> Denver charges a small monthly OPT on people who earn $500 or more in a month working in the city. It usually shows up on W-2 paychecks, but self-employed workers should check whether it applies to them.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Rules change. Check Denver Business Licensing and the state's MyBizColorado site before you sell products, hire help or host guests. A quick call costs less than a fine.</div>

